  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Result:** two `{test}` references cover the example's scenario and outcomes. One assertion is weaker than ideal (see the last section).\n\n**Setup matches the scenario.** `fixture()` in `tests/definitions.rs` (about lines 8–36) builds:\n- requirement `rules.ready`, which cites `{term}glossary.a`;\n- definition `a`, whose body cites `glossary.b`;\n- definition `b`, whose body cites `glossary.a`.\n\nThat is the R → A → B → A cycle the example describes.\n\n**Test `cyclic_dependencies_are_captured_once_and_changes_require_review`** (`tests/definitions.rs`, line 50 onward):\n- **Termination and one input per definition.** It captures evidence, which returns, so collection terminates. It asserts `record[\"inputs\"].len() == 3`, i.e. R, A and B once each.\n- **Stability.** Recapturing leaves the file byte-identical. Re-expansion therefore doesn't duplicate inputs.\n- **Moving a definition.** Prefixing the chapter with blank lines still validates cleanly. This matches the rule that moving an unchanged definition must not invalidate its snapshot.\n- **Phrase change.** Changing `:phrase: beta` to `new beta` produces an \"input text changed\" error that names a `glossary.` definition. The record is not silently refreshed, since the file is unchanged after validation.\n- **Body change.** Changing \"See\" to \"Read\" produces the same error and the same non-refresh.\n- **Recapture.** It resets `determination` to false and keeps 3 inputs, and approval then validates cleanly.\n- **Missing and duplicate inputs.** Removing `glossary.b`'s input yields \"glossary.b, found 0\". Duplicating an input yields \"found 2\". Recapturing repairs the record to 3 inputs. This covers the dependencies requirement's \"exactly one input\" rule and its reporting of missing inputs. It also covers the inputs requirement's rejection of changed snapshots.\n\n**Test `rust_item_inputs_capture_terms_without_counting_them_as_citations`:**\n- **Transitive capture.** A Rust item that cites only `glossary.a` still yields definition inputs for both `glossary.a` and `glossary.b`. This is transitive capture through the cycle for a second input kind.\n- **Change detection on B.** Changing only B's phrase (`beta` to `changed`) yields an \"input text changed\" error that names `evidence/rules.ready.json` and `glossary.b`. This directly shows that a change to B's phrase invalidates R's snapshot of B.\n\n**Minor weakness.** The body-change case in the first test replaces \"See\" in both A and B. Its assertion only checks for `glossary.`, so it doesn't show on its own that a change to B's body invalidates B's snapshot. B's phrase change is shown precisely, and body changes use the same snapshot-text comparison. I judge this acceptable, and it is not acknowledged by any FIXME.",
